{
  "requirement": "The `habeas` CLI MUST use `clap` to define and parse its commands and options.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the requirement is met. The CLI is defined and parsed with `clap`, and nothing else parses arguments.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `Cargo.toml` depends on `clap` 4.6.7 with the `derive` feature.\n- `src/main.rs:10` marks `struct Cli` with `{impl}` for this requirement. It derives `clap::Parser` and sets `#[command(name = \"habeas\")]`.\n- The `Command`, `MdbookCommand` and `HookCommand` enums derive `Subcommand`. They define every command and option, using `ArgGroup` for the exclusive and required constraints.\n- `src/main.rs:90` also carries an `{impl}` marker. `main` calls `Cli::parse()`.\n- A search of `src` found no `std::env::args` or other manual argument parsing. All argument handling goes through `clap`, so no part of the requirement lacks an `{impl}` marker.\n- The other behavior in `main` (workspace loading, status, author, and so on) is marked as implementing other requirements, so it is not unspecified extra behavior for this one.\n\n**Tests**\n- No test is tagged `{test}` for `requirements.cli.clap`.\n- `tests/cli.rs` runs the real binary with an unknown command (`unknown`, `agents`) and with invalid `setup` options (`--wrong`, `--install-skills`). It asserts a failure and a `clap` usage message (\"Usage: habeas\") on stderr. That exercises `clap`'s parse-error behavior end to end, though the tests are tagged for `cli.invocation`.\n- The requirement is a structural statement that a library is used. Its use is plain from the source, so a dedicated test would add little.",
  "inputs": [
